# Tailwind Only — No Inline Styles

Use **Tailwind CSS utility classes** for all styling. Do **not** use the `style` attribute (inline styles) in components.

## Rules

1. **Use Tailwind classes** for layout, spacing, colors, typography, borders, and responsive behavior.
2. **Do not use `style={{ }}`** or the HTML `style` attribute. If you need a value Tailwind doesn’t expose (e.g. a one-off CSS variable), use a Tailwind arbitrary value or extend the theme instead of inline styles.
3. **Prefer `className`** for all styling. Avoid importing separate CSS modules or global styles for component-level styling unless the rule explicitly allows it.

## Examples

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — inline style
<div style={{ marginTop: 16, color: '#333' }}>Content</div>

// ✅ GOOD — Tailwind
<div className="mt-4 text-gray-800">Content</div>
```

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — style object
<span style={{ fontSize: '0.875rem', fontWeight: 600 }}>Label</span>

// ✅ GOOD — Tailwind
<span className="text-sm font-semibold">Label</span>
```

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — mixing style with className
<button className="rounded" style={{ padding: '8px 16px' }}>Submit</button>

// ✅ GOOD — all Tailwind
<button className="rounded px-4 py-2">Submit</button>
```

## Exceptions

- **Third-party components** that require a `style` prop for their API: pass through only when the library docs require it.
- **Dynamic values** that cannot be expressed with Tailwind (e.g. `width: ${percent}%`): prefer Tailwind arbitrary values (e.g. `className={\`w-[${percent}%]\`}`) or a small utility in your Tailwind config; use inline `style` only as a last resort and add a short comment explaining why.

## When Editing

- Replace any new or existing `style` usage with equivalent Tailwind classes.
- Prefer standard Tailwind tokens (e.g. `mt-4`, `text-sm`, `bg-primary`) over arbitrary values when possible.
